        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which person or organization did Ranjan Sarukkalige work for?\n\nAnswer A: University of Glasgow\n  about: Ranjan Sarukkalige\n  sense: employment\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Sarukkalige's academic career began in 2001 when he joined the University of Glasgow as a research assistant.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Curtin University\n  about: Ranjan Sarukkalige\n  sense: employment\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"According to his profile on ResearchGate, a social networking site for scientists and researchers, Sarukkalige has listed Curtin University as one of his affiliations.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Ranjan Sarukkalige\n\nRanjan Sarukkalige is a renowned researcher who made significant contributions to the field of interdisciplinary studies. Born in 1975, Sarukkalige pursued his academic career at the University of Glasgow, one of the oldest and most prestigious institutions in the United Kingdom. Early Life and Education\n\nSarukkalige was born in Glasgow, Scotland, to a family of academics. He developed an interest in research at a young age and went on to pursue his undergraduate degree in sociology from the University of Glasgow. During his time at the university, he was heavily influenced by the works of prominent sociologists and anthropologists, which shaped his research interests. Career\n\nSarukkalige's academic career began in 2001 when he joined the University of Glasgow as a research assistant. He worked under the guidance of Dr. Sophia Patel, a prominent sociologist, on a project focused on urbanization and its impact on community development. This project led to the publication of several papers in reputed journals, including the Journal of Urban Studies and the International Journal of Community Development. In 2005, Sarukkalige was appointed as a lecturer in the Department of Sociology at the University of Glasgow. He taught courses on research methods, social theory, and urban sociology, and supervised several Ph.D. students. During this period, he also collaborated with researchers from other universities on projects related to migration, identity, and cultural diversity. Notable Research Contributions\n\nSarukkalige's most notable research contribution is his work on the intersection of culture and identity in urban spaces. His 2010 paper, \"Negotiating Identity in Multicultural Cities,\" published in the Journal of Cultural Studies, is widely cited and has been influential in shaping the field of urban sociology. In 2012, Sarukkalige was awarded a grant from the Economic and Social Research Council (ESRC) to conduct a study on the impact of gentrification on community cohesion. The study, titled \"Gentrification and Community Change,\" was published in 2015 and received widespread media attention. Awards and Honors\n\nSarukkalige has received several awards and honors for his contributions to the field of sociology. In 2015, he was awarded the Philip Abrams Memorial Prize for his work on urban sociology. He has also been a visiting fellow at the University of California, Berkeley, and the University of Melbourne.\nDocument 2: Ranjan Sarukkalige\n\nRanjan Sarukkalige is a researcher who has made significant contributions to his field of study. One of his notable affiliations is with Curtin University, a prestigious university located in Perth, Western Australia. Career\n\nRanjan Sarukkalige has had a distinguished career as a researcher, and his work has been recognized and respected by his peers. One of the institutions he has been associated with is Curtin University, where he has worked as a researcher. Affiliation with Curtin University\n\nThere is conclusive evidence to suggest that Ranjan Sarukkalige has worked for Curtin University. According to his profile on ResearchGate, a social networking site for scientists and researchers, Sarukkalige has listed Curtin University as one of his affiliations. [1] Furthermore, a search of Curtin University's staff directory reveals that Sarukkalige has been employed by the university as a researcher. [2]\n\nAdditionally, several academic papers co-authored by Sarukkalige have been published in reputable journals, with his affiliation listed as Curtin University. [3][4] These papers demonstrate his expertise in his field and provide further evidence of his association with the university. References\n\n[1] ResearchGate. (n.d.). Ranjan Sarukkalige. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.researchgate.net\\/profile\\/Ranjan_Sarukkalige>\n\n[2] Curtin University. (n.d.). Staff Directory. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/staff.curtin.edu.au\\/staff-directory\\/>\n\n[3] Sarukkalige, R., et al. (2020). Title of Paper. Journal of Research, 10(2), 123-135. doi: 10.1016\\/j.jr.2020.02.001\n\n[4] Sarukkalige, R., et al. (2019). Title of Paper. International Journal of Science, 8(1), 56-67. doi: 10.1016\\/j.ijos.2019.01.002\n\nNote: The references provided are fictional and used only for demonstration purposes.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" 